        194,
        193,
        150,
        915
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(a) -ω/ομαι verbs are called ‘thematic’, because they have a stem, e.g. παυ-, to which they add a ‘thematic’ vowel -ο- or -ε-, and an ending, e.g. -μεν, to give e.g. παύομεν ‘we stop’ (see 16(b), 52(b), 102(c)).",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "box",
      "order": 3,
      "bbox": [
        140,
        91,
        295,
        915
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "But δίδωμι has no thematic vowel: it is therefore ‘athematic’.\nWhat this means is that the endings go straight onto the stem without any intervening vowel.\nIn other words, the stem is διδο- in the present and δο- in the past.\nThe same is true for -μι verbs in general. Simply add endings and enjoy.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": "bullet",
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 4,
      "bbox": [
        310,
        194,
        344,
        915
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(b) Note how the 2s. middle endings (-μαι) -σαι (-ται) and (-μην) -σο (-το) appear in full for the first time (but cf. 177).",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 5,
      "bbox": [
        353,
        194,
        441,
        915
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(c) Given that one keeps a firm grip on these stems (διδο-, δο-), there is very little here that is difficult to recognise. The most remarkable feature is the aorist inflection with its change from ἔ-δω-κα, -κας, -κε to ἔ-δο-μεν, -τε, -σαν in the plural. For the full conjugation, see 376, and cf. 426–7.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
